.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1228 PM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

The Red Flag Warning for the North Bay Interior Mountains was
allowed to expire at 11 AM this morning as winds speeds continue to
diminish. However, critical to near critical fire weather conditions
prevail across the higher elevations of the Bay Area and north
Central Coast through Saturday due to low relative humidity values.

Today remains on track to be the warmest day of the week as high
pressure dominates the weather pattern aloft with areas of Moderate
HeatRisk, especially near the coast. Interior areas will peak in
the upper 80s to lower 90s. Coastal areas will remain mild to very
warm in the 70s to mid 80s as weak onshore winds are forecast by
early afternoon.

Tonight, lower elevations are forecast to cool into the 50s to lower
60s under mostly clear skies. The higher elevations are once again
forecast to be warm and only cool into the 70s to lower 80s
(attributing to the low relative humidities).

For Saturday, an increase in onshore winds and lack of offshore flow
will result in a slight cooling trend in most areas. However, areas
of Moderate HeatRisk are forecast to continue with temperatures
forecast to be some 10-20 degrees F above seasonal averages.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1228 PM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

More of a zonal flow returns to the region on Sunday. This will
result in temperatures cooling to near seasonal norms with the
return of a marine layer and increased onshore winds. Upper level
troughing deepens off of the West Coast through early next week,
resulting in below average temperatures returning to the region. A
cut-off low is forecast to develop off of the Bay Area and north
Central Coast by Tuesday and is currently projected to push inland
across southern California by Wednesday afternoon/evening. This will
bring a slight chance to chance of rainfall to the region Tuesday
through early Thursday morning. There is also generally a less than
15% chance of thunderstorm on Tuesday when the core of the cut-off
low remains just off of the Central Coast. However, the trajectory
of the cut-off low will largely impact which areas receive the most
rainfall and/or any thunderstorm development. Thus, uncertainty
remains relatively high. So be sure to check back on the forecast as
we will likely gain more clarity in the forecast over the coming
days.

What is most clear, the cool down! As the 500 mb clusters generally
agree on the troughing off of the West Coast through much of next
week. As best said by my colleague, "Bottom line: expect a pattern
change to occur next week with below normal temperatures, potential
for light rain, breezy to gusty onshore winds, and a low potential
for thunderstorms."

